{"timestamp":"2026-09-07T04:35:00.411Z","ordinal":17799,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_02be90ffdd5dff4a016a9e3ec652f487d0938f9173cbd1f625","status":"completed","call_id":"call_729toXz2Hkj3hkg5qlY5jeGj","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /Users/jdx/src/blog-dotfiles-history/content/posts/2026-09-05-dotfiles-that-save-themselves.md\\n@@\\n Then just edit `.zshrc`. Use your editor, `sed`, or an agent. mise saves your changes automatically. No commit command or extra step after each edit.\\n+\\n+Tracking is explicit. Referencing a template or managing a file doesn't enroll it, and mise doesn't automatically track its own configuration. Track a directory to include its descendants, subject to exclusions.\\n@@\\n-Your configuration and template sources live on an ordinary Git branch; eligible backups of each machine's file history go to the same origin too, encrypted if you ask for it. You can still clone the repository and make normal Git commits, but mise handles routine saving and sharing.\\n+There's one ordinary Git history. Each save commits the tracked files, and sync pushes those same commits. Manual sync delays pushing, not saving: the intermediate commits go along when you eventually sync. mise keeps the bare repository separate from your live files, so there's no `.git` directory in your home directory. You can still clone the repository and make normal Git commits.\\n+\\n+Every tracked file is eligible to reach origin, including its earlier committed versions. Don't track files you never want there. Untracking stops future capture and leaves the live file alone; it doesn't erase old commits. Without an origin, the repository stays local.\\n@@\\n [dotfiles]\\n \\\"~/.zshrc\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\" }\\n \\\"~/.config/nvim\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\" }\\n+\\\"~/.config/mise/config.toml\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\" }\\n@@\\n-Bootstrap brings down the shared setup, previews conflicts with existing files, and applies the selected configuration. Existing machine-local overrides stay local.\\n+Bootstrap brings down the shared setup, previews conflicts with existing files, and applies the selected configuration. The explicit config entry above is what brings the tool, package, and service declarations along. Tracking `.zshrc` alone restores `.zshrc`, not the rest of your setup.\\n@@\\n-Sharing and backup are separate choices too:\\n-\\n-```toml\\n-[dotfiles]\\n-\\\"~/.ssh/config\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\", share = false }\\n-\\\"~/.config/private-app\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\", share = false, backup = false }\\n-```\\n-\\n-The first keeps local history and remains eligible for remote backup, but isn't applied as shared configuration on other machines. The second stays in local history only.\\n-\\n@@\\n-One public-recipient list covers all encrypted files. Each listed machine can decrypt independently with its own identity. mise encrypts before publishing and decrypts before applying; applications, template rendering, and local history still use plaintext. Filenames and configuration remain visible, so keep secrets out of inline configuration and use a private repository. A file that cannot be unlocked pauses applying and publishing for the whole setup while fetching and local history continue.\\n+One public-recipient list covers all encrypted files. Each listed machine can decrypt independently with its own identity. mise encrypts before the contents enter Git—not just before pushing—and decrypts when restoring the live file. Filenames remain visible. Publication checks the complete history: encrypting today's version doesn't make yesterday's plaintext commit safe to share.\\n@@\\n-You can use ordinary age or SSH keys, or install age plugins for a Mac's Secure Enclave and Linux TPM. Hardware keys stay bound to their devices; keep an independent recovery recipient in case a device is lost. An interactive pull may request Touch ID or a PIN. Once decrypted, local history and caches remain readable without unlocking the hardware again.\\n-\\n-Machine recovery backups have their own recipients and can also be encrypted:\\n-\\n-```sh\\n-mise bootstrap dotfiles origin set git@github.com:jdx/dotfiles.git --encrypt-backups\\n-```\\n-\\n-Every checkpoint this machine backs up then goes to the repository as one [age](https://age-encryption.org) payload, encrypted for the identities on the machine: its age key, or its SSH public key, or a key mise generates when there is neither. `--recipient` selects an explicit recipient list instead of those defaults. Only the machine name, checkpoint id, and time stay visible; file names, descriptions, and content don't. Another machine restores those backups with any matching identity. Identity files are never captured or shared; provision them separately. Files marked encrypted are excluded from plaintext backups. Changing a policy later doesn't erase old commits or other people's clones.\\n+You can use ordinary age or SSH keys, or install age plugins for a Mac's Secure Enclave and Linux TPM. Hardware keys stay bound to their devices; keep an independent recovery recipient in case a device is lost. An interactive pull may request Touch ID or a PIN. Provision identities separately; they don't belong in the tracked repository.\\n@@\\n \\\"~/.zshrc\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\" }\\n-\\\"~/.gitconfig\\\" = { source = \\\"templates/gitconfig.tera\\\", mode = \\\"template\\\" }\\n+\\\"~/templates\\\" = { mode = \\\"track\\\" }\\n+\\\"~/.gitconfig\\\" = { source = \\\"~/templates/gitconfig.tera\\\", mode = \\\"template\\\" }\\n@@\\n-The template lives in your setup repository. When you run `mise bootstrap`, mise renders it into `~/.gitconfig` using that machine's settings.\\n+Tracking `~/templates` puts the template sources in your setup repository. When you run `mise bootstrap`, mise renders the template into `~/.gitconfig` using that machine's settings. The generated file isn't tracked unless you explicitly enroll it too.\\n@@\\n-I'd keep secrets out of shared dotfiles. Use [mise's environment secrets](https://mise.jdx.dev/environments/secrets/) or [fnox](https://fnox.jdx.dev/) when an application can read them from the environment. If it needs a plaintext credential file, provision that separately and keep it out of shared history. Encrypted files can carry those contents through sync, but their live copies and local history remain plaintext. Configuration itself stays readable on the shared branch.\\n+I'd keep secrets out of dotfiles where possible. Use [mise's environment secrets](https://mise.jdx.dev/environments/secrets/) or [fnox](https://fnox.jdx.dev/) when an application can read them from the environment. Encrypted tracking can carry credential files through sync, but their live copies are still plaintext. Keep secrets out of inline configuration.\\n*** End Patch\"));\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Update File: /Users/jdx/.codex/worktrees/dotfiles-one-history/mise/e2e/cli/test_dotfiles_encrypted_capture\\n@@\\n assert_contains \\\"git --git-dir=$repo show HEAD:home/.private-config | head -1\\\" 'mise-encrypted-file-v1'\\n+assert_succeed \\\"mise bootstrap dotfiles undo --yes\\\"\\n+assert \\\"cat ~/.private-config\\\" 'second-private-value'\\n+assert_fail \\\"git --git-dir=$repo cat-file -e $first_plain\\\"\\n+assert_fail \\\"git --git-dir=$repo cat-file -e $second_plain\\\"\\n*** End Patch\"));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a079e2-ddee-7920-adc1-13c441666749","create_time":1788755651.367051}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-07T04:35:00.423Z","ordinal":17800,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a07177-9855-71a2-89cd-4a9a37cdbfcb","turn_id":"01a079e2-ddee-7920-adc1-13c441666749","item":{"type":"FileChange","id":"exec-386ccdb3-ad72-4fe7-89f9-ac884e1761e1","changes":{"/Users/jdx/src/blog-dotfiles-history/content/posts/2026-09-05-dotfiles-that-save-themselves.md":{"type":"update","unified_diff":"@@ -56,2 +56,4 @@\n \n+Tracking is explicit. Referencing a template or managing a file doesn't enroll it, and mise doesn't automatically track its own configuration. Track a directory to include its descendants, subject to exclusions.\n+\n ## Autosave, like a video game\n@@ -116,3 +118,5 @@\n \n-Your configuration and template sources live on an ordinary Git branch; eligible backups of each machine's file history go to the same origin too, encrypted if you ask for it. You can still clone the repository and make normal Git commits, but mise handles routine saving and sharing.\n+There's one ordinary Git history. Each save commits the tracked files, and sync pushes those same commits. Manual sync delays pushing, not saving: the intermediate commits go along when you eventually sync. mise keeps the bare repository separate from your live files, so there's no `.git` directory in your home directory. You can still clone the repository and make normal Git commits.\n+\n+Every tracked file is eligible to reach origin, including its earlier committed versions. Don't track files you never want there. Untracking stops future capture and leaves the live file alone; it doesn't erase old commits. Without an origin, the repository stays local.\n \n@@ -148,2 +152,3 @@\n \"~/.config/nvim\" = { mode = \"track\" }\n+\"~/.config/mise/config.toml\" = { mode = \"track\" }\n \n@@ -159,3 +164,3 @@\n \n-Bootstrap brings down the shared setup, previews conflicts with existing files, and applies the selected configuration. Existing machine-local overrides stay local.\n+Bootstrap brings down the shared setup, previews conflicts with existing files, and applies the selected configuration. The explicit config entry above is what brings the tool, package, and service declarations along. Tracking `.zshrc` alone restores `.zshrc`, not the rest of your setup.\n \n@@ -207,12 +212,2 @@\n Sync publishes the saved version, not whatever happens to be on disk. An unrelated autosave doesn't accidentally scoop up those unsaved changes.\n-\n-Sharing and backup are separate choices too:\n-\n-```toml\n-[dotfiles]\n-\"~/.ssh/config\" = { mode = \"track\", share = false }\n-\"~/.config/private-app\" = { mode = \"track\", share = false, backup = false }\n-```\n-\n-The first keeps local history and remains eligible for remote backup, but isn't applied as shared configuration on other machines. The second stays in local history only.\n \n@@ -228,13 +223,5 @@\n \n-One public-recipient list covers all encrypted files. Each listed machine can decrypt independently with its own identity. mise encrypts before publishing and decrypts before applying; applications, template rendering, and local history still use plaintext. Filenames and configuration remain visible, so keep secrets out of inline configuration and use a private repository. A file that cannot be unlocked pauses applying and publishing for the whole setup while fetching and local history continue.\n-\n-You can use ordinary age or SSH keys, or install age plugins for a Mac's Secure Enclave and Linux TPM. Hardware keys stay bound to their devices; keep an independent recovery recipient in case a device is lost. An interactive pull may request Touch ID or a PIN. Once decrypted, local history and caches remain readable without unlocking the hardware again.\n-\n-Machine recovery backups have their own recipients and can also be encrypted:\n-\n-```sh\n-mise bootstrap dotfiles origin set git@github.com:jdx/dotfiles.git --encrypt-backups\n-```\n+One public-recipient list covers all encrypted files. Each listed machine can decrypt independently with its own identity. mise encrypts before the contents enter Git—not just before pushing—and decrypts when restoring the live file. Filenames remain visible. Publication checks the complete history: encrypting today's version doesn't make yesterday's plaintext commit safe to share.\n \n-Every checkpoint this machine backs up then goes to the repository as one [age](https://age-encryption.org) payload, encrypted for the identities on the machine: its age key, or its SSH public key, or a key mise generates when there is neither. `--recipient` selects an explicit recipient list instead of those defaults. Only the machine name, checkpoint id, and time stay visible; file names, descriptions, and content don't. Another machine restores those backups with any matching identity. Identity files are never captured or shared; provision them separately. Files marked encrypted are excluded from plaintext backups. Changing a policy later doesn't erase old commits or other people's clones.\n+You can use ordinary age or SSH keys, or install age plugins for a Mac's Secure Enclave and Linux TPM. Hardware keys stay bound to their devices; keep an independent recovery recipient in case a device is lost. An interactive pull may request Touch ID or a PIN. Provision identities separately; they don't belong in the tracked repository.\n \n@@ -256,6 +243,7 @@\n \"~/.zshrc\" = { mode = \"track\" }\n-\"~/.gitconfig\" = { source = \"templates/gitconfig.tera\", mode = \"template\" }\n+\"~/templates\" = { mode = \"track\" }\n+\"~/.gitconfig\" = { source = \"~/templates/gitconfig.tera\", mode = \"template\" }\n ```\n \n-The template lives in your setup repository. When you run `mise bootstrap`, mise renders it into `~/.gitconfig` using that machine's settings.\n+Tracking `~/templates` puts the template sources in your setup repository. When you run `mise bootstrap`, mise renders the template into `~/.gitconfig` using that machine's settings. The generated file isn't tracked unless you explicitly enroll it too.\n \n@@ -265,3 +253,3 @@\n \n-I'd keep secrets out of shared dotfiles. Use [mise's environment secrets](https://mise.jdx.dev/environments/secrets/) or [fnox](https://fnox.jdx.dev/) when an application can read them from the environment. If it needs a plaintext credential file, provision that separately and keep it out of shared history. Encrypted files can carry those contents through sync, but their live copies and local history remain plaintext. Configuration itself stays readable on the shared branch.\n+I'd keep secrets out of dotfiles where possible. Use [mise's environment secrets](https://mise.jdx.dev/environments/secrets/) or [fnox](https://fnox.jdx.dev/) when an application can read them from the environment. Encrypted tracking can carry credential files through sync, but their live copies are still plaintext. Keep secrets out of inline configuration.\n \n","move_path":null}},"status":"completed","stdout":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM /Users/jdx/src/blog-dotfiles-history/content/posts/2026-09-05-dotfiles-that-save-themselves.md\n","stderr":""},"started_at_ms":1788755700422,"completed_at_ms":1788755700423}}
